00:00:34.802 --> 00:00:39.113
You may not want to think about it this way,
but your mouth is really just one giant,

00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:41.239
wet cave for microbes.

00:00:41.239 --> 00:00:46.280
Sure, it’s also a mouth, which means that it’s
a part of a body that does all the mouth

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.379
things that the body needs it to do.

00:00:48.379 --> 00:00:51.000
It bites and digests and tastes.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.040
But from the perspective of bacteria like
these, your mouth is not a tool.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.270
It's a home.

00:00:57.270 --> 00:01:00.844
It's a place that provides shelter and food,

00:01:00.844 --> 00:01:03.780
but it is also a place that 
can pose many threats.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:09.060
And the interplay between our mouths and the
microbes that take up residence within them

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.117
ends up, inevitably, affecting our own health.

00:01:12.534 --> 00:01:16.612
We’ll start this episode with a similar
disclaimer to the one we made when we talked

00:01:16.612 --> 00:01:18.320
about dust mites in your home.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:25.956
While watching this video, you may find yourselves
suddenly gripped by a desire to clean.

00:01:25.956 --> 00:01:30.173
Only this time, instead of seeking out a broom
to flush the dust mites out of your home,

00:01:30.173 --> 00:01:32.840
you might find yourself reaching for
a toothbrush.

00:01:33.375 --> 00:01:37.092
So it is with that in mind that we need to
extend a big thank you to Ela,

00:01:37.092 --> 00:01:39.649
a friend of our master of microscopes.

00:01:39.649 --> 00:01:45.305
Ela kindly ate a lot of sugary food and skipped
brushing her teeth for two days

00:01:45.305 --> 00:01:47.430
to make this video possible.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:52.909
When Ela texted James that quote, “My mouth is
totally gross and perfect for sampling,”

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:54.910
the result was this:

00:01:54.910 --> 00:02:01.112
tartar and plaque scraped off her gums and
the spaces between her teeth.

00:02:01.112 --> 00:02:07.340
Within these samples, you can see dense populations
of bacteria, some of which move around quite

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.499
a bit, like the corkscrew-shaped 
spirochetes here.

00:02:11.499 --> 00:02:16.287
As is so often the case, the microscopist
Antonie van Leeuwenhoek got there

00:02:16.287 --> 00:02:18.000
long before we did.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:23.424
In 1677, he published a paper 
documenting the “little animalcules”

00:02:23.424 --> 00:02:25.510
he observed under the microscope.

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:31.458
Among his many illustrations and descriptions
is a set of drawings depicting the bacteria

00:02:31.458 --> 00:02:33.585
that came from his own mouth.

00:02:36.044 --> 00:02:40.676
In 1889, a dentist named Willoughby Dayton
Miller published the book

00:02:40.676 --> 00:02:44.236
“Die Mikroorganismen der Mundhöhle,”

00:02:44.236 --> 00:02:48.154
which was translated and published 
a year later under the English title 

00:02:48.154 --> 00:02:50.631
“The Micro-Organisms of the Human Mouth: 

00:02:50.631 --> 00:02:53.893
The  Local and General Diseases 
Which Are Caused by Them”.

00:02:54.362 --> 00:02:56.584
In the first chapter, he wrote:

00:02:56.584 --> 00:03:02.100
It has been established beyond all question
that myriads of micro-organisms are constantly

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:07.489
present in the human mouth, and that these,
under favorable circumstances are capable

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:14.159
of manifesting an action of the utmost significance
upon the local as well as the general health

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:15.362
of the patient.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:23.920
This statement would be the foundation of
a major shift in our understanding of tooth decay.

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.130
At the time there was a theory that tooth decay 
was caused by some kind of wiggling worm

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.630
that needed to be driven out of our mouths.

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:37.209
But Miller developed an alternative explanation
called chemo-parasitic theory, where the bacteria

00:03:37.209 --> 00:03:45.071
in our mouths ferment sugars, but in the process
release acids which then cause our teeth to decay.

00:03:45.071 --> 00:03:48.142
And humans had been 
cleaning their teeth for millenia,

00:03:48.142 --> 00:03:51.462
including with ancient tools like porcupine quills.

00:03:51.462 --> 00:03:56.840
But the chemo-parasitic theory gave a more
specific—and accurate—framework to think

00:03:56.840 --> 00:04:01.750
about how we clean our teeth, and it would
shape much of how we approached dental disease

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:09.488
in the 20th century, including brushing your teeth, 
flossing, and the addition of fluoride to toothpaste.

00:04:09.488 --> 00:04:14.569
But while Miller’s work helped us uncover
the idea of microbes as a mass interacting

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:19.130
with our teeth, it turns out that understanding
the specific roles of individual microbes

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.049
within that mass is much more complicated.

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:23.816
To understand how complicated,

00:04:23.816 --> 00:04:28.853
we only need to look at two of the 
protists we've found on Ela’s teeth.

00:04:28.853 --> 00:04:34.259
The first is Trichomonas tenax, the gourd-shaped
creature swimming here.

00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:39.541
Trichomonas tenax likes to take up residence
in the teeth, gums, tongue, and saliva.

00:04:39.541 --> 00:04:44.289
And it is associated with several diseases
in the body, but it’s not clear if it’s

00:04:44.289 --> 00:04:49.180
involved at all in periodontal disease, which
you can think of as diseases that involve

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:51.220
inflammation of the gums.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.660
Scientists have been finding Trichomonas tenax
in samples that come from patients who have

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:01.165
periodontal disease since the 1960s, but that is
not the same as finding out that the microbe

00:05:01.165 --> 00:05:03.345
itself is causing disease.

00:05:04.094 --> 00:05:10.090
And then there’s the blob-shaped Entamoeba
gingivalis, which was the first amoeba found

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:11.680
inside of humans.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.592
It has a name that you might immediately associate
with gingivitis, and for good reason:

00:05:16.592 --> 00:05:21.060
this protist is found in high numbers in people
with periodontitis.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:27.736
But again, that doesn’t necessarily mean
this protist actually causes the disease.

00:05:27.736 --> 00:05:30.940
It might be that these protists are involved
in the disease.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:36.389
Or it might be that the changes in the mouth
that lead to periodontitis also make for a

00:05:36.389 --> 00:05:42.020
comfortable home from the perspective of Trichomonas
tenax and Entamoeba gingivalis.

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:48.620
The oral microbiome is one of the largest
and most diverse microbiomes in your bodies,

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.905
second only to the microbiome of our guts.

00:05:51.905 --> 00:05:59.178
And every day that we eat, drink, brush, and floss 
brings changes to the environment they live in—

00:05:59.178 --> 00:06:03.020
some of which are too large for the microbial
communities to weather.

00:06:03.458 --> 00:06:09.660
We started by describing your mouth as a big,
moist cave for microbes, and the comparison

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:16.111
holds when you think of the complex and mysterious
ecosystems that live within caves.

00:06:16.111 --> 00:06:21.849
There are enigmatic creatures that latch onto
walls and swim within the waters, that hide

00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:25.320
themselves and shape the environment around
them.

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:28.634
But for the organisms of the 
cave of your mouth, well,

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:35.002
we feed them and we nourish them  and 
then we destroy them because we have to,

00:06:35.002 --> 00:06:40.621
never fully knowing what it is that we have 
washed down the sink.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:52.000
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

